<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.75%" pitch="-3.10%">
            Now Isaac had come from Beer Lahai Roi, and he dwelt in the South.<break time="500ms"/>
      He went out to meditate in the field in the evening.<break time="500ms"/>
      The sun was low, the light golden and warm across the open land.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Isaac walked alone in the quiet, perhaps praying, perhaps remembering his mother Sarah who had died.<break time="500ms"/>
      He did not know what was coming toward him along the road.<break time="500ms"/>
      He only knew the stillness of that evening field.<break time="1800ms"/>
      And he lifted his eyes and looked, and there, in the distance, camels were coming.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Rebekah also lifted her eyes, and when she saw Isaac, she dismounted from her camel.<break time="500ms"/>
      She said to the servant, who is this man walking in the field to meet us?<break time="500ms"/>
      The servant said, it is my master.<break time="500ms"/>
      So she took a veil and covered herself.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Two people, strangers until this moment, brought together across hundreds of miles by a prayer at a well and a father's faith.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The servant told Isaac all the things he had done.<break time="500ms"/>
      Every detail of the journey, the prayer, the answered kindness, and the willing heart of Rebekah.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Then Isaac brought her into his mother Sarah's tent.<break time="500ms"/>
      He took Rebekah, and she became his wife, and he loved her.<break time="500ms"/>
      And Isaac was comforted after his mother's death.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The tent that had been empty was filled again.<break time="500ms"/>
      The grief that had lingered found its gentle answer.<break time="500ms"/>
      Not through force or hurry, but through the slow faithfulness of God, who brings the right people together in the fullness of time.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You can rest in that same faithfulness now.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>
